        "blocking": 0,
        "total_blocking": 0
      },
      "body": "### Discussed in https://github.com/iovisor/bpftrace/discussions/2730\r\n\r\n<div type='discussions-op-text'>\r\n\r\n<sup>Originally posted by **fxv2** August 30, 2023</sup>\r\n@htbegin Great thanks for the implementing of the kernel space memory watchpoint. And I'm hoping to get some help about the usage in the arm64 enviroment.\r\n# Question:\r\nDoes bpftrace  kernel space memory watchpoint support the arm64 architecture?\r\nAm I using the kernel space memory watchpoint function in the right way?\r\n\r\n# the arm64 ubuntu22 in qemu, kernel 5.10\r\nbpftrace v0.14.0\r\n## watchpoint of jiffies\r\nwhen running `bpftrace -e \"watchpoint:0x$(awk '$3 == \"jiffies\" {print $1}' /proc/kallsyms):8:w {@[kstack] = count();}\"\r\n` the CPU usage reaches 99%,( knowing this by the qemu graphical monitor) causing the system to become unresponsive, requiring a reboot. \r\n## Using linux device driver\r\nA Linux device driver was developed to validate the int32 watchpoint functionality. The `increment_value ` is modified every 3 seconds,  but the bpftrace commands are not being triggered.\r\n\r\nthe command line results:\r\n``` text\r\n[  546.165405] Incremented value at address 00000000d9e28f2a to 46\r\n[  549.164762] Incremented value at address 00000000d9e28f2a to 47\r\nalex@debian:~/kernel/bpf_watchpoint_test_module$ sudo bpftrace -e 'watchpoint:0x00000000d9e28f2a:4:w { printf(\"hit!\\n\"); exit(); }'\r\nAttaching 1 probe...\r\n^C\r\n```\r\n\r\nmy_module.c\r\n``` C\r\n#include <linux/init.h>\r\n#include <linux/module.h>\r\n#include <linux/kernel.h>\r\n#include <linux/ktime.h>\r\n#include <linux/hrtimer.h>\r\n\r\nMODULE_LICENSE(\"GPL\");\r\nMODULE_AUTHOR(\"Your Name\");\r\nMODULE_DESCRIPTION(\"Increment Driver for ARM64\");\r\nMODULE_VERSION(\"0.1\");\r\n\r\nstatic int increment_value = 0;\r\nstatic struct hrtimer increment_timer;\r\nstatic ktime_t interval;\r\n\r\nenum hrtimer_restart increment_timer_callback(struct hrtimer *timer) {\r\n    increment_value++;\r\n    printk(KERN_INFO \"Incremented value at address %p to %d\\n\", &increment_value, increment_value);\r\n\r\n    hrtimer_forward_now(timer, interval);\r\n    return HRTIMER_RESTART;\r\n}\r\n\r\nstatic int __init increment_driver_init(void) {\r\n    interval = ktime_set(3, 0); // 3 seconds interval\r\n\r\n    hrtimer_init(&increment_timer, CLOCK_MONOTONIC, HRTIMER_MODE_REL);\r\n    increment_timer.function = increment_timer_callback;\r\n\r\n    hrtimer_start(&increment_timer, interval, HRTIMER_MODE_REL);\r\n\r\n    printk(KERN_INFO \"Increment driver loaded\\n\");\r\n    return 0;\r\n}\r\n\r\nstatic void __exit increment_driver_exit(void) {\r\n    hrtimer_cancel(&increment_timer);\r\n    printk(KERN_INFO \"Increment driver unloaded\\n\");\r\n}\r\n\r\nmodule_init(increment_driver_init);\r\nmodule_exit(increment_driver_exit);\r\n```\r\n# On an ARM64 Ubuntu 20 development board,\r\nbpftrace v0.18.0-107-g08a4 (build from git)\r\n\r\n`bpftrace -e \"watchpoint:0x$(awk '$3 == \"jiffies\" {print $1}' /proc/kallsyms):8:w {@[kstack] = count();}\"`\r\n\r\n executing the  command triggers the watchdog, leading to a system reboot.</div>",
