      ],
      "milestone": null,
      "comments": 2,
      "created_at": "2025-10-26T22:42:29Z",
      "updated_at": "2025-10-27T16:38:18Z",
      "closed_at": null,
      "assignee": null,
      "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
      "issue_field_values": [

      ],
      "type": {
        "id": 27188688,
        "node_id": "IT_kwDOCWTeF84Bnt3Q",
        "name": "RFC",
        "description": "Request for comments - issues that don't outline a specific task or bug but rather serve as a means for discussion",
        "color": "green",
        "created_at": "2025-06-05T03:52:23Z",
        "updated_at": "2025-06-05T03:52:23Z",
        "is_enabled": true
      },
      "active_lock_reason": null,
      "sub_issues_summary": {
        "total": 0,
        "completed": 0,
        "percent_completed": 0
      },
      "issue_dependencies_summary": {
        "blocked_by": 0,
        "total_blocked_by": 0,
        "blocking": 0,
        "total_blocking": 0
      },
      "body": "As more bpf.c files get added to the standard library each time bpftrace is invoked these files need to parsed, compiled, BTF extracted, and linked. This will start to have a negative affect on startup latency (though more testing/benchmarking is needed to find out how much).\n\nThere are (at least) two options, which aren't mutually exclusive:\n- conditionally include bpf.c files based on static analysis of the program ([Example PR](https://github.com/bpftrace/bpftrace/pull/4743))\n- cache compilation of these bpf.c files ([Example PR](https://github.com/bpftrace/bpftrace/pull/4749))\n\nI'm personally in favor of starting with option 1 and adding caching as a next layer (as needed) but we want something that is less cumbersome than the option in the example PR (basically keeping a map of external function to bpf.c file). Perhaps adding a simple namespacing like syntax when calling external stdlib functions, e.g., `usdt::__usdt_arg(...)` which indicates we need to import `usdt.bpf.c` or `util/strings::__str_contains(...)` to import `util/strings.bpf.c`. Most (if not all) of these external stdlib bpf.c calls will be hidden from the user as they are wrapped in macros so they can be a little verbose.\n\nOther ideas/suggestions are welcome. Mostly creating this issue as a tracking task.",
      "reactions": {
        "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/bpftrace/bpftrace/issues/4777/reactions",
        "total_count": 0,
