      "body": "This is a sub-proposal for #3773. It is intended to hash out namespaces per the [comment](https://github.com/bpftrace/bpftrace/pull/3781#issuecomment-2662978463) on the POC.\n\nFirst, some basic requirements for namespaces:\n* Provide a simple mechanism to avoid ambiguity as programs get larger.\n* Provide a way to clearly distinguish external types and maps from internal types, if desired.\n\nAnd some anti-requirements:\n* Don't add significant complexity or cognitive overhead.\n* Be familiar to users of namespaces in other languages (e.g. C++), but don't over-index on these.\n\n# Basics\n\nFor simplicity, I propose no explicit `namespace` keywords or `using` keywords. The namespace for an identifier is specified using C++ syntax. This is to avoid ambiguity for field access. For example, an identifier `foo` in the `std` namespace can be specified as `std::foo`.\n\nNamespaces are defined implicitly based on the script or import **directory**. The directory is chosen to allow users the flexibility of not using namespaces, or easily combining multiple separate objects into a single namespace, or adding directory structure to avoid ambiguity. For external, a user may prefer to add their external BPF programs into an `extern` directory, and then `import \"extern/thing1\"` or `import \"extern/thing2\"` as needed. Or they may prefer to simply ship their script and external object together, and `import \"thing1\"`.\n\nImports will be resolved using two paths:\n* First, the directory that contains the script itself will be checked. Only directories that are not world-writable will be walked.\n* Second, bpftrace may have embedded file contents that match the given import paths.\n\nAdditional paths can be added via a command-line option or via an environment variable.\n\nNamespaces apply to all objects that may be imported: external maps, functions, and macros.\n\n## Default namespace\n\nThe `bpftrace` script always lives in the \"default\" namespace, which is effectively the empty string. Therefore `foo` and `::foo` are equivalent. While this may seem insignificant, it is a useful mechanism to resolve ambiguity. User types for each probe will be prioritized when resolving types. These are not namespaced, and therefore `foo` can resolve to a user type, while `::foo` will resolve to the `bpftrace` type.\n\nThe standard library also lives in the \"default\" namespace. By default, a special module named `builtins` will be imported (unless a `--nostdlib` option is provided). Since `builtins` does not have a directory component, there is no special handling; this be imported into the default namespace. Over time, this import may contain other imports and macros to wrap calls to other namespaces, etc.\n\n## Other namespaces\n\nAs noted above, namespaces are strictly a result of the **directory** that a given import lives in.\n\nConsider the following:\n```\nimport \"foo\"\nimport \"bar/foo\"\n```\n\nThis will process two imports, and one will be available in the \"default\" namespace, and the next will be available in the `bar` namespace.\n\nImport statements are always absolute, and will always resolve given the order above. This eliminates some classes of ambiguity regarding the structure (did you mean subdirectory foo? Or top-level module foo?). It also ensures that namespaces are used consistently, even within imported code. `bar::baz` always means the same thing, regardless of where it is called from.\n\nNote that namespaces are also nested. Consider the following:\n```\nimport \"bar/baz/foo\"\n```\n\nThis imports into the `bar::baz` namespace, and would therefore require calls like `bar::baz::foo`. Note that within the implementation, this namespace will be mangled as \"bar/baz\", but the syntax must use `::` separators, not directory separators.",
      "reactions": {
