      "body": "Currently, user mode stack walking (ustack()) relies on frame pointers to be present, which is rarely the case. So ustack() in its current state often produces incomplete stacks.\nThere are a few tickets related to this:\n* https://github.com/bpftrace/bpftrace/issues/1006\n* https://github.com/bpftrace/bpftrace/issues/1744\n* https://github.com/iovisor/bcc/issues/1234\n\nA possible solution would be to parse the eh_frame/debug_frame sections of the binary and all loaded libs, convert them to tables and push as maps to an eBPF program that does the stack walking.\nThe tables can be kept pretty compact without sacrificing direkt access to each row, as opposed to walking the full function as in dwarf.\nThis approach also makes it possible to restore the registers at each frame, giving access to the function arguments. This should be optional, as it comes with a higher runtime cost during stack walking.\n\nThis has been done before, for example here\nhttps://www.polarsignals.com/blog/posts/2022/11/29/dwarf-based-stack-walking-using-ebpf\nAlthough they don't restore all registers.\n\nA different solution would be to just copy the user stack and do the walking in user space, as danobi suggested here\nhttps://dxuuu.xyz/stack-symbolize.html. As user stacks can grow pretty large, this approach imho is not feasible for high frequency probes. It also eliminates the possibility to further process the stack inside the probe.\n\nThe kernel approach with ORC is also similar.\n\nUpsides:\n * proper stack frames in most environments\n * stack frames with arguments possible\n\nDownsides:\n  If the user works in an environment with full frame pointers, using eh_frame information would slow down stack walking. So we should give the user the choice to solely rely on frame pointers.\n\nImplementation:\nMy idea is to provide the table builder as a standalone library, along with some bpf code, so that it can be used by more than just bpftrace. To find the best way to integrate this into bpftrace I might need some advice/discussion.\n\nThe current status is that I have a prototype that works well as a standalone program.\n",
