      "type": {
        "id": 82713,
        "node_id": "IT_kwDOCWTeF84AAUMZ",
        "name": "Feature",
        "description": "A request, idea, or new functionality",
        "color": "blue",
        "created_at": "2024-01-24T13:01:17Z",
        "updated_at": "2024-10-08T08:29:56Z",
        "is_enabled": true
      },
      "active_lock_reason": null,
      "sub_issues_summary": {
        "total": 0,
        "completed": 0,
        "percent_completed": 0
      },
      "issue_dependencies_summary": {
        "blocked_by": 0,
        "total_blocked_by": 0,
        "blocking": 0,
        "total_blocking": 0
      },
      "body": "This is a fork off of the discussion from #342 @danobi \r\n\r\nThe idea here is to use the existing CI build for bpftrace to generate various release targets when the release process is initiated by `git tag`. \r\n\r\nThese artifacts should be attached to a Release object from the github API transparently. \r\n\r\nIt may also make sense to use this process to drive publishing artifacts to other systems, such as:\r\n\r\n- Publishing a debian package to an iovisor-hosted apt repo\r\n- Publishing a flatpak or snapcraft image\r\n- Pushing a docker image to quay.io/iovisor/bpftrace or dockerhub\r\n\r\nI think that this wolud help to make bpftrace more accessible.\r\n\r\nThis issue mostly supposes that #1041 makes sense, and bits like authenticating with quay.io/iovisor images would help to speed up CI runs, which is itself a concern of #1041 \r\n\r\n# Implementation\r\n\r\nMost of this comes down to someone with credentials on the various package publishing system to encrypt them and store them in the travis.yml\r\n\r\nTravis itself provides deploy hooks that are probably flexible enough to initiate almost any process.\r\n\r\nMost of these artifacts already exist, and deploy steps just need to be configured to copy them or do some packaging post-processing.\r\n\r\n# Side benefit - Faster CI builds\r\n\r\nIf we can get access to push images from previous runs, we only rebuild layers when necessary. Each build type can have their own docker tag based on their ENV vars. This could help drop CI times significantly, depending on the docker pull speed.\r\n\r\n",
      "reactions": {
