      "body": "> NOTE: this proposal is inspired and informed heavily by #3610. This is a large over-arching proposal, which will require multiple sub-proposals and implementation steps. This lays out the high-level concept and some of the details, but is not intended to be a complete answer to every future question.\n\nCurrently, bpftrace suffers from a few challenges:\n\n* Advanced use cases can quickly hit barriers in terms of functionality. \n* Development velocity is slow due to the cross-cutting nature of changes, and the degree to which bespoke IR generation is required for most things.\n\nIn order to solve these problems, it will be useful to introduce the ability to dynamically extend the functionality of bpftrace by writing plain C, BPF C, or additional bpftrace scripts. Over time, internal functionality that is considered part of the “standard library” should migrate to these mechanisms, leaving only minimal core functionality where bespoke IR generation and plumbing is needed.\n\nI propose adding a new statement, “import”:\n\n```\nimport “dir/basename”\n```\n\nThis statement will cause bpftrace to look for three different categories of extension. At least one of these must be present, and many cases will have more than one:\n* A host file: `dir/basename.c`\n  * Alternately, the user may provide `dirname/basename.so` to avoid compilation in the hot path. **This file must have BTF types attached already (e.g. pahole -J `dir/basename.so` when building).**\n* A BPF file: `dir/basename.bpf.c`\n  * Alternately, the user may provide `dirname/basename.bpf.bc` to avoid compilation in the hot path (but still allow full inlining)\n  * Alternately, the user may provide `dirname/basename.bpf.o` to support arbitrary BPF programs (without inlining)\n* A bpftrace file: `dir/basename.bt`\n\nThe host file can be used to provide dynamic user-space runtime behavior and types. The BPF can be used to provide bespoke BPF helper functions as well as pre-defined probes and maps. The bpftrace file can be used to provide user-defined bpftrace functions, as well as import additional modules. Each of these mechanisms is described in depth below.\n\nExplicitly this proposal has no namespaces for imported extensions. This is to avoid the complexity (which is typically needed only for large-scale code bases) and more easily enable core extensions and internal types/functions to shift to using this model.\n\nIn the future, extensions can also be found in an archive file, e.g. `foo.zip`, which would be evaluated as though it was available in the local source directory. This simplifies distribution.\n\nThe details of each of these is described here, followed by a proposed implementation.\n\n# Overview\n\nWhen the import statement is found, the imported module will be evaluated (recursively, so leafs are ultimately instantiated first – cycles are not allowed).\n\nFor the bpftrace files, these are evaluated and loaded into the ASTContext. For example, configuration blocks are appended recursively in a depth-first manner, as are subprograms and probes. There is no specific intermediate format for compiled extensions. (If needed, you can distribute extensions as a `.zip` file, and use the `.so` and `.bpf.bc` formats described below to avoid higher costs for loaded extensions.)\n\nFor host field and BPF files, these are generally handled as:\n* If a source file is available:\n  * An in-memory version of the source file is generated which also contains necessary suitable BTF-generated type definitions for all currently known types (from the host layer or the BPF layer).\n  * Common & necessary headers are automatically made available from embedded versions to eliminate the need for any system headers (e.g. vendored bpf.h). This allows the extensions to be built using e.g. `BPF_CORE_READ` and suitable annotations are injected.\n* If either the source was generated above, or the serialized bytecode was available, then the source is compiled or the bytecode deserialized. This is all done with the existing embedded libclang. This module can be queried and either:\n    * Modified and linked (Linker.linkModuleIn) into the current llvm.Module (for the BPF code).\n    * Modified and compiled to either a shared object or as part of the emitted AOT binary. The shared object is subsequently loaded dynamically via `dlopen` and symbols found via `dlsym`.\n* Type information and function information is extracted (either from the `llvm.Module` or via the embedded BTF in the binary) and used internally for compiling the bpftrace program.\n* After the bpftrace program has been compiled and code generated, the associated `llvm::Module`s for the imported extensions are appropriately linked (as a dynamic object, or in the LLVM-sense for the BPF code) to the current module.\n\n# Existing maps and probes\n\nWhen the `.bpf.c` source file is first transformed into an `llvm::Module` we will scan for all global variables in a `.maps` section and suitably register these as `@name` values usable by all probes. This can also be done with a binary file via the BTF information. (It is not clear whether we will always convert to BTF first, but the same concept applies either way.) This is the “escape hatch” that can be used to define individual maps with whatever types you like – using the BPF C syntax. These variables will be linked in at the end and usable by the generated programs.\n\nSimilarly, existing probes are scanned by looking at standard BPF section names. During attach time, these probes will be attached to the noted attach points.\n\n# The standard library\n\nEventually, we can embed a set of files for a standard library within bpftrace that are parsed and loaded through this extension mechanism. For example, consider: `std/core.c`, `std/core.bpf.c` and `std/core.bt` files and an implicit `import “std/core”` in every program. These could be broken up into different components (and `std/core.bt` could load those), or parts of the standard library could be optional and loaded manually (e.g. `import “std/python”`).\n\nOver time many of the internal types and details can be moved to a combination of bpftrace user-defined functions, native BPF code, and C helper code. This relies on the asynchronous function extensibility and type extensibility describe below, each of which will require richer proposals.\n\n# Asynchronous functions\n\n> This will be refined and implemented as part of a later proposal. This is a sketch for how this will relate to the extensions.\n\nAll exported (non-static) functions in the host file are selected. These are new asynchronous function targets. bpftrace assigns each of these functions a unique identifier for dispatch and adds the functions to a large dispatch table (this dispatch table must be serialized for the AOT case). Note that the names for these functions are suitably mangled such that they are resolvable in the standard way during AST passes and analysis.\n\nWhen reloading the final linked shared object, each of these symbols is resolved within the dispatch table. (It is fine to use `dlsym` even if the symbol is in the main binary.) A final transformation pass is applied to the BPF code (at the LLVM level, since it must include extern IR as well) that transforms a call to a function of this name to the bytecode that pushes the asynchronous call to the ring buffer.\n\n# Types\n\n> This will be refined and implemented as part of a later proposal. This is a sketch for how this will relate to the extensions.\n\nIn reality, types in bpftrace exist in two different worlds (BPF and userspace) and need not be exactly the same. The use of types is technically isolated within each extension, e.g. a `foo.c` file may define a `struct foo` and use it freely within its own functions. The complexity arises when the functions in the BPF world would like to pass these types to the user space helpers (which at least is a one-way transformation).\n\nCustom types can be defined by the existence of a function named `serialize_` in the BPF source file, and functions prefixed `deserialize_`, `free_` (*), `emit_raw_` and `emit_json_` in the C source file with the same name. These functions will be automatically called to serialize and deserialize these types when making asynchronous calls (e.g. as part of the IR generated with the call replacement noted above), and will be used to back the `print` implementation.\n\n(*) The `free_` method will be called with the returned value (register only) when the value is discarded. This allows dynamic memory management to be used for the serialized types, supporting e.g. proper strings in userspace.\n\nThe named types that can be used in probes are defined from the types defined in the BPF extensions. For example, a kernel probe will have `builtin + stdlib + custom_includes + extensions+ vmlinux` types available. A module would have `builtin + stdlib + custom_includes + extensions+ vmlinux + module` types. A user probe would have `builtin + stdlib + custom_includes + extensions + types_from_debuginfo` available. Currently, all probes have the same set of types available, and refining this to be a union over a specific set is a future proposal.\n\n# Implementation steps (sketch)\n\nGeneral structure:\n* For module loading:\n  * If `.bpf.c` is available and is not older than a `.bpf.bc` module, then compile that to LLVM bytecode.\n    * If LLVM bytecode is available and is not older than a `.bpf.o`, then compile the bytecode to an object.\n    * Pull BTF information from the `bpf.o`, which must always be available if any of the above are available.\n  * For final compilation:\n    * If LLVM bytecode is available, inline all functions and link them into module.\n    * Otherwise, probes are loaded and linked as BPF objects directly from the object file.\n * Start with the simplest case: import “.o” and load existing maps and tracepoints.\n* Next, proceed to support additional cases and refine iteratively.\n",
