      "locked": false,
      "assignees": [

      ],
      "milestone": null,
      "comments": 1,
      "created_at": "2026-05-06T00:00:24Z",
      "updated_at": "2026-05-12T00:07:20Z",
      "closed_at": "2026-05-12T00:07:18Z",
      "assignee": null,
      "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
      "issue_field_values": [

      ],
      "type": null,
      "active_lock_reason": null,
      "draft": false,
      "pull_request": {
        "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/bpftrace/bpftrace/pulls/5148",
        "html_url": "https://github.com/bpftrace/bpftrace/pull/5148",
        "diff_url": "https://github.com/bpftrace/bpftrace/pull/5148.diff",
        "patch_url": "https://github.com/bpftrace/bpftrace/pull/5148.patch",
        "merged_at": "2026-05-12T00:07:18Z"
      },
      "body": "Stacked PRs:\n * #5152\n * __->__#5148\n\n\n--- --- ---\n\n### Don't error on integer sign mismatch\n\n\nThis error has been a constant source of pain for users and is not worth\nthe possible prevention of strange behavior.\n\nInstead replace errors with a simple warning when we create an implicit\ncast that may cause truncation, reinterpretation, or undefined behavior.\n\nThe general idea here is that we don't want bpftrace to get in the way of\nits users. Many dynamically typed languages (e.g. Python and Javascript)\ndon't even support unsigned integers because of the problems they cause.\n\nThis will also remove the pain of a recent breaking change to update the\ntypes of integer literals and subtraction/decrement operations:\nhttps://github.com/bpftrace/bpftrace/pull/5138\n\nThis is an example that was previously invalid:\n```\n$a = (uint64)1;\n$a = (int64)2;\n```\nNow the resulting type of `$a` is an int64 and an implicit cast (with a\nwarning) is inserted at the first assignment: `$a = (int64)(uint64)1;`.\n\nThis will happen for map values/keys, scratch variables, and binops.\nIt is also applied to integers nested in tuples and records.\n\nThe behavior for flexible_sign types or positive integer literals is not\nchanged.\n\nAdditionally, there is no longer an error for variable declarations:\n```\n$b = -1;\nlet $a: uint16 = $b;\n```\nA cast will be inserted in front of `$b` to `uint16` and a warning will\noccur.\n\nHowever! If we can easily prove statically that an integer literal can not\nfit into a specific type then there is an error. For example these are\nerrors:\n```\nlet $a: uint16 = -1;\nlet $b: uint8 = 1000000000000;\n$x = -1;\n$x = 10223372036854775807;\n```\n\nAs usual, warnings can be silenced with the `--no-warnings` flag.\n\nSigned-off-by: Jordan Rome <linux@jordanrome.com>\nSigned-off-by: Jordan Rome <linux@jordanrome.com>",
      "reactions": {
        "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/bpftrace/bpftrace/issues/5148/reactions",
