      "body": "## Summary\r\n\r\nFixes issue #4767 where while loops using map variables in their condition were being rejected by the BPF verifier as infinite loops, causing confusing runtime errors.\r\n\r\n## Problem\r\n\r\nThe BPF verifier cannot statically prove that loops are bounded when the condition depends on map variables (`@var`) because:\r\n- Map variables can be modified by other programs or threads\r\n- The verifier cannot track all possible modifications to map values\r\n- This results in cryptic \"infinite loop detected\" errors at BPF load time\r\n\r\nExample that was failing:\r\n```bash\r\nbegin { @a = 0; while (@a < 1024) { print(1); ++@a; } }\r\n```\r\n\r\nSolution\r\nThis PR adds semantic analysis to detect this pattern early and provide clear guidance:\r\n\r\nEarly Detection: Added [MapVariableDetector](vscode-file://vscode-app/snap/code/211/usr/share/code/resources/app/out/vs/code/electron-browser/workbench/workbench.html) class using the Visitor pattern to identify map variable usage in while loop conditions\r\nClear Error Message: Provides actionable feedback suggesting the use of local variables instead\r\nComprehensive Coverage: Detects both simple map variables (@var) and map access expressions (@[\"key\"])\r\n",
      "reactions": {
        "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/bpftrace/bpftrace/issues/4834/reactions",
