      },
      "body": "bpftrace crashes with a SEGFAULT when printing a linear histogram that contains only values in the final overflow bucket.\n\n**Steps to reproduce:**\n(records a 150B write into an `lhist` with a max 100 range)\n\n```bash\n# bash -c \"sleep 1; exec dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/null bs=150 count=1 status=none\" &\n# pid=$!\n# build/src/bpftrace -e \"tracepoint:syscalls:sys_enter_write /pid == $pid/ { @bytes = lhist(args->count, 0, 100, 10); }\"\nAttached 1 probe\n^C\n\n@bytes:\nSegmentation fault\n```\n\nThe root cause is that the `TextEmitter<Value::Histogram>::emit` logic does not handle this overflow-only scenario.\n\nThe histogram building logic `build_linear_histogram`, contains an optimization to skip leading empty buckets.\n\n```gdb\nThread 1 \"bpftrace\" hit Breakpoint 1, bpftrace::build_linear_histogram (\n    values=std::vector of length 1002, capacity 1002 = {...}, min=0, max=100, step=10)\n(gdb) list\n400       output::Value::Histogram hist;\n401       auto [min_index, max_index] = indices(values);\n          ...\n405       if (min_index != 0) {\n406         hist.lower_bound = lhist_index_label(min + ((min_index - 1) * step), step);\n407       }\n408\n409       for (int i = min_index; i <= max_index; i++) {\n410         // The final bucket represents values over the label, and we encode\n411         // this into the histogram structure by simply omitting the label.\n412         if (min + (i * step) <= max) {\n413           hist.labels.push_back(lhist_index_label(min + (i * step), step));\n414         }\n415         hist.counts.push_back(values.at(i));\n416       }\n417       return hist;\n(gdb) next ...\n417       return hist;\n(gdb) p hist\n$1 = {lower_bound = std::optional = {[contained value] = {\n      variant = std::variant [index 2] = {100}}},\n  labels = std::vector of length 0, capacity 0,\n  counts = std::vector of length 1, capacity 1 = {1}}\n```\n\nThis results in a `Value::Histogram` with a `lower_bound` but an empty `labels` vector, as shown in the GDB output.\n\nThe crash occurs at `TextEmitter<Value::Histogram>::emit`, since it assumes that if `lower_bound` exists, `labels` is not empty, and it accesses `labels[0]` without a check.\n\n```cpp\nstruct TextEmitter<Value::Histogram> {\n  static void emit(std::ostream &out, const Value::Histogram &hist)  {\n    ...\n    for (size_t i = 0; i < hist.counts.size() || i < hist.labels.size(); i++) {\n      ...\n      if (i == 0) {\n        if (!hist.lower_bound) {\n          ...\n        } else if (single_value(*hist.lower_bound, hist.labels[i])) {\n          header << \"[\";\n          TextEmitter<Primitive>::emit(header, *hist.lower_bound);\n          header << \"]\";\n        } else {\n          header << \"[\";\n          TextEmitter<Primitive>::emit(header, *hist.lower_bound);\n          header << \", \";\n          TextEmitter<Primitive>::emit(header, hist.labels[i]);\n          header << \")\";\n        }\n```\n\n\n## Proposal\n\n`TextEmitter<Value::Histogram>::emit` should be enhanced to correctly interpret this overflow only situation.\nThe proposed change adds a condition to `src/output/text.cpp` to detect when `lower_bound` exists but `labels` is empty, and then formats the output correctly.\n\n```cpp\n// in template <> struct TextEmitter<Value::Histogram>\n\nif (i == 0) {\n  if (!hist.lower_bound) {\n    ...\n  } else if (hist.labels.empty()) { // Oveflow only case\n    header << \"[\";\n    TextEmitter<Primitive>::emit(header, *hist.lower_bound);\n    header << \", ...)\";\n  } else if (single_value(*hist.lower_bound, hist.labels[i])) {\n    ...\n}\n```\n\n\n```bash\n# bash -c \"sleep 1; exec dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/null bs=150 count=1 status=none\" &\n# pid=$!\n# build/src/bpftrace -e \"tracepoint:syscalls:sys_enter_write /pid == $pid/ { @bytes = lhist(args->count, 0, 100, 10); }\"\nAttached 1 probe\n^C\n\n@bytes:\n[100, ...)             1 |@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@@|\n```\n\nThis resolves the bug by making the Histogram TextEmitter to cover overflow-only case, without modifying the existing data creation logic.\n",
