        "merged_at": "2025-04-17T19:31:38Z"
      },
      "body": "Stacked PRs:\n * #3998\n * #3997\n * __->__#3996\n\n\n--- --- ---\n\n### refactor: move to static types\n\n\nThis change makes AST node consistent, and adopts static types rather\nthan relying on RTTI.\n\nThe major motivation for this is to move away from the `Expression`\ninheritence model, whereby instances reach into the `Expression` base\nclass and access the `SizedType` directly. Instead, a layer of\nabstraction is added here to allow individual node to provide access to\ntheir type information via a returned reference. Instead of being\nspecifically a `SizedType` object, this reference can be an abstract\ntype of some kind, e.g. originating outside of our own type system.\n\nConsider a future pass which examines `Call` nodes, and replaces them\nwith `ExternalCall` nodes based on the resolve imports. The types for\nthose new `ExternalCall` nodes should be BTF types sourced from the\nimpored type information. Currently we would need to figure out a way to\nconvert every type exhaustively. While this is done currently within the\n`BTF` class, it is all driven by the `ClangParser`. Reinvoking the\nparser again and again in order to introduce these types to our unified\ntype universe is not ideal: we should be able to operate exclusively and\ndirectly on the type information contained in the external objects. Over\ntime, our internal implementations will leverage these mechanisms,\nallowing us to remove the special cases from `SizedType`.\n\nWith this change, individual node are still typed, but they have their\nown field, appropriately named. Direct access to these fields are\nencouraged, and this is where type inference will operate. This reduces\nambiguity, as node without fundamental type information (e.g. a\nPositionalParameter, a Block, etc.) need not store an invalid value.\nThey can implement a `type` function which simply references to the\nappropriate sub-type.\n\nNote that the `type` method returns immutable references, which means\nthat type inference and code generation can only mutate types on node\nthat it understands, which is a desirable safety property. This is on\ntop of the additional safety properties that no more `static_cast`s or\n`dynamic_cast`s are permitted, reducing the possibility of subtle memory\nerrors.\n\nMost of this change itself is boilerplate in two forms:\n* Making AST node consistent in structure and moving the `type`.\n* Changing all call-sites to use this new consistent structure.\n\nThere should be no semantic change.\n\nSigned-off-by: Adin Scannell <amscanne@meta.com>\n",
      "reactions": {
        "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/bpftrace/bpftrace/issues/3996/reactions",
